Horologium Telluric

Ingredients: PROGRESSIVE HOUSE + NEW WAVE + COZY, WARM & CHILL LO-FI + Bells  
Creative Catalyst: Musical instruments handcrafted from nickel-iron meteorite fragments and 18th-century brass clockwork.

Horologium Telluric strips away the sterile, endless builds of traditional progressive house and replaces them with an intimate, domestic warmth. The driving 4/4 club pulse is muted—filtered through thick wool felt and recorded on degraded cassette tape—while soaring New Wave basslines are played on heavy, metallic tongues forged from iron meteorites. Piercing synth leads are completely abandoned in favor of intricate, cascading clockwork bell chimes that wind down, slip gear-teeth, and re-sync in real time. It sounds like a secret dance party taking place inside a sunlit, antique clockmaker’s attic while cosmic debris rains softly onto the roof.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 118–122 BPM. The classic progressive house kick is softened into a dusty, heart-like thump, swung subtly against the mechanical, jittery tick-tock of brass gear-escapements instead of hi-hats.
*   Harmonic Palette: Nostalgic Dorian and Mixolydian modes driven by chorus-heavy, fretless synth-basses. Warm, tape-saturated electric pianos provide cozy lo-fi chords that melt into shimmering, microtonal bell overtones.
*   Production Signatures: 
       Gated Escapement Reverb:* Snares and brass clicks hit an artificial chamber plate that suddenly snaps shut on exact quarter-note divisions, mimicking a mechanical pendulum lock.
       Stellar Hiss Bias:* A steady, comforting low-fidelity background hum created by running high-gain magnetic tape over unpolished meteorite dust.
       Chronometric Phase-Shifting:* An impossible production technique where two identical bell melodies are routed through physical mechanical gears; as the gears heat up from friction, the tracks micro-drift out of phase before violently snapping back into alignment on the 32nd bar.
*   Dynamics: Anti-climactic builds. Rather than explosive drops, tracks expand through the gradual addition of layered, winding clockwork mechanisms and warm bass swells, then gently "unwind" into silent, ambient tape-hiss.

Genre Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the dark winters of Black Forest horology towns, Horologium Telluric was ignited when rogue clockmakers and exiled synth-pop producers began melting down celestial iron meteorites to forge custom gongs, balance springs, and bells. Dubbed "Tick-Dancers," the subculture gathers in defunct watch factories and glass greenhouses converted into low-lit tea dens. The fashion is defined by heavy wool overcoats lined with metallic foil, velvet waistcoats fitted with exposed brass gears, and oxidized silver thimbles worn on every finger to play raw meteorite plates during live sets. Rather than raucous raving, the ritual involves communal, rhythmic swaying on thick oriental rugs, drinking hot spiced botanical infusions, and listening for the exact moment the music’s mechanical gears lose momentum.

Typical Instruments
*   Meteorite Tongue Gongs & Chimes: Custom idiophones milled from extraterrestrial nickel-iron, producing unnaturally long, cold, sub-atomic resonance.
*   Mechanical Clockwork Bell-Carillon: An automated rig of antique brass bells driven by escapement wheels and mechanical cranks, replacing modern sequencers.
*   Chorus-Soaked Fretless Bass Synth: A vintage New Wave analog bass synth routed through analog bucket-brigade chorus pedals for a rubbery, post-punk drive.
*   Upright Cassette Felt Piano: An upright piano muffled with triple-layered felt, fed through a dual-capstan cassette deck for warm, pitch-warped lo-fi chords.

For Fans Of: The Durutti Column, Tycho, Manuel Göttsching, Royksopp

